blocks.body:
A category $\mathcal{C}$ consists of

1. a collection of objects $X, Y, Z, \dots$;
2. for each pair of objects a set $\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(X, Y)$ of morphisms (arrows) $f\colon X \to Y$;
3. a composition rule assigning to $f\colon X \to Y$ and $g\colon Y \to Z$ a morphism $g \circ f\colon X \to Z$;
4. for each object an identity morphism $\mathrm{id}_X\colon X \to X$,

such that composition is associative, $(h \circ g) \circ f = h \circ (g \circ f)$, and identities are neutral, $f \circ \mathrm{id}_X = f = \mathrm{id}_Y \circ f$ for every $f\colon X \to Y$.

A morphism $f\colon X \to Y$ is an isomorphism if there is $g\colon Y \to X$ with $g \circ f = \mathrm{id}_X$ and $f \circ g = \mathrm{id}_Y$. Objects are only ever studied up to isomorphism.
